00:01:41.663 --> 00:01:43.457
Laurie, thanks for joining us.

00:01:43.457 --> 00:01:46.193
- Thanks so much for having me on.

00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:48.929
- So maybe a good place
to start, your class,

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:53.526
Psychology and the Good
Life, what is that about?

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:56.923
And why do you feel that
it needs to be taught?

00:01:56.923 --> 00:01:58.473
- Yeah, yeah well, the
class really started

00:01:58.473 --> 00:01:59.863
when I took on a new role at Yale.

00:01:59.863 --> 00:02:01.063
So I'm a professor at Yale.

00:02:01.063 --> 00:02:03.153
I've been teaching
there for over a decade,

00:02:03.153 --> 00:02:04.233
but in just the last couple years,

00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:06.683
I took on this new role as
head of college on campus.

00:02:06.683 --> 00:02:08.323
So, Yale was one of these strange schools

00:02:08.323 --> 00:02:09.683
like Hogwarts in Harry Potter,

00:02:09.683 --> 00:02:11.777
where there's like these
colleges within the college.

00:02:11.777 --> 00:02:13.563
I'm head of Silliman College,

00:02:13.563 --> 00:02:15.763
but what that means is that I
live on campus with students.

00:02:15.763 --> 00:02:17.163
I'm like with them in the trenches,

00:02:17.163 --> 00:02:19.723
like eating with them in the
dining hall and hanging out.

00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:21.063
And honestly, when I took on the new role,

00:02:21.063 --> 00:02:22.113
I didn't like what I was seeing.

00:02:22.113 --> 00:02:24.173
I was seeing this college
student mental health crisis

00:02:24.173 --> 00:02:25.689
up close and personal
with so many students

00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:28.230
reporting feeling depressed and anxious,

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.363
or just like stressed out about life.

00:02:30.363 --> 00:02:32.793
And fast-forwarding, I'd
have so many conversations

00:02:32.793 --> 00:02:34.013
with students where I'm
like, how's it going?

00:02:34.013 --> 00:02:35.893
They're like, oh, if I could
just get through midterms,

00:02:35.893 --> 00:02:37.673
or, oh, if I could just
like get to spring break.

00:02:37.673 --> 00:02:40.173
it was like not being
present and enjoying things.

00:02:40.173 --> 00:02:43.063
And so it made me realize that
just needed some practical

00:02:43.063 --> 00:02:45.083
strategies for like how to feel better

00:02:45.083 --> 00:02:46.263
in the modern day and age.

00:02:46.263 --> 00:02:48.303
And so the class really
emerged out of that.

00:02:48.303 --> 00:02:49.773
It's a psychology class.

00:02:49.773 --> 00:02:52.793
It's like a lot of introductory
psychology material,

00:02:52.793 --> 00:02:54.633
but it's all about the
science of wellbeing.

00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:56.263
It's like evidence based strategies

00:02:56.263 --> 00:02:57.673
you can use to feel better.

00:02:57.673 --> 00:02:59.203
And the best part about the
classes is the homework,

00:02:59.203 --> 00:03:01.815
which wasn't the typical,
like y'all class homework.

00:03:01.815 --> 00:03:04.713
It was homework like
meditate or make a new social

00:03:04.713 --> 00:03:07.143
connection or savor your
next eating experience.

00:03:07.143 --> 00:03:10.203
It was homework designed to put
those practices into effect.

00:03:10.203 --> 00:03:12.273
And so now I put this class together

00:03:12.273 --> 00:03:13.463
like a normal Ivy league class,

00:03:13.463 --> 00:03:15.923
just assuming, you know, 30
or 40 students would take it.

00:03:15.923 --> 00:03:18.653
That's pretty typical for
a psychology class here.

00:03:18.653 --> 00:03:19.683
Was a little astounded

00:03:19.683 --> 00:03:21.701
when over a thousand students enrolled.

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:24.063
In the end, we had a quarter
of the Yale student body

00:03:24.063 --> 00:03:25.223
tried to take the class,

00:03:25.223 --> 00:03:27.883
which showed me the students
are voting with their feet.

00:03:27.883 --> 00:03:29.163
You know, they don't like this culture

00:03:29.163 --> 00:03:30.783
of feeling academically stressed

00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:31.793
and overwhelmed all the time.

00:03:31.793 --> 00:03:33.893
And I think they really
wanted some evidence based

00:03:33.893 --> 00:03:36.703
strategies they could use to feel better.

00:03:36.703 --> 00:03:37.536
- Now on one level,

00:03:37.536 --> 00:03:39.253
what you just said is
surprising that it's so popular,

00:03:39.253 --> 00:03:40.191
but on another level,

00:03:40.191 --> 00:03:43.573
it's so obvious that it should be that way

00:03:43.573 --> 00:03:45.653
because, what are we doing anything for?

00:03:45.653 --> 00:03:47.603
Why are we even living our lives

00:03:47.603 --> 00:03:49.579
if not to have a good life,

00:03:49.579 --> 00:03:52.773
to be happy and to be in the
moment and to be present.

00:03:52.773 --> 00:03:54.903
So I have a ton of questions

00:03:54.903 --> 00:03:57.263
and already questions
are starting to come in.

00:03:57.263 --> 00:03:59.299
I'm curious, two things.

00:03:59.299 --> 00:04:02.633
One is, do you think that
things are different now

00:04:02.633 --> 00:04:04.093
than they were when I said,

00:04:04.093 --> 00:04:06.253
I think I look a lot older than you.

00:04:06.253 --> 00:04:08.493
When I was gonna say, when
you and I went to school,

00:04:08.493 --> 00:04:11.253
I'm amazed to meet very
accomplished professors

00:04:11.253 --> 00:04:12.849
who at least look a lot younger than me.

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:13.963
(laughing)

00:04:13.963 --> 00:04:15.593
But you think things are different

00:04:15.593 --> 00:04:18.233
than when we went to
school or generations past.

00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:21.913
And then what are the main tools

00:04:21.913 --> 00:04:22.943
that you're trying to get folks

00:04:22.943 --> 00:04:24.123
to help deal with that?

00:04:24.123 --> 00:04:25.173
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:25.173 --> 00:04:26.583
Well, there's some data on this.

00:04:26.583 --> 00:04:28.293
I mean, the self reported rates

00:04:28.293 --> 00:04:31.503
of mental health issues have
been skyrocketing, right?

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:33.483
And we could say that maybe
that's because there was more

00:04:33.483 --> 00:04:35.333
stigma to admitting you're depressed

00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:36.953
or anxious or something
when we were in school.

00:04:36.953 --> 00:04:38.753
But those reasons have been skyrocketing

00:04:38.753 --> 00:04:40.773
in just the last five years, right?

00:04:40.773 --> 00:04:42.573
So I don't think since 2016,

00:04:42.573 --> 00:04:44.903
the stigma has gone down
that much about saying

00:04:44.903 --> 00:04:47.003
I'm feeling anxious or feeling depressed.

00:04:47.003 --> 00:04:48.621
But we're seeing things like (inaudible).

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.523
- In 2016, that's a short time period

00:04:50.523 --> 00:04:52.696
till now you seeing data.

00:04:52.696 --> 00:04:54.130
So sorry, I interrupted.

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:54.963
- No, no, no.

00:04:54.963 --> 00:04:56.085
It's crazy.

00:04:56.085 --> 00:04:57.823
The rates are skyrocketing
since 2009, right?

00:04:57.823 --> 00:04:59.233
Just about 10 years,

00:04:59.233 --> 00:05:01.553
double the rates of depression
in college aged students.

00:05:01.553 --> 00:05:02.386
Terrible, right?

00:05:02.386 --> 00:05:04.823
I mean, so these things are going up fast.

00:05:04.823 --> 00:05:06.343
The million dollar question is why?

00:05:06.343 --> 00:05:08.743
And the honest answer is
that we just don't know.

00:05:08.743 --> 00:05:10.613
There's probably a lot
of factors going on.

00:05:10.613 --> 00:05:12.733
You know, we're more
socially disconnected.

00:05:12.733 --> 00:05:15.683
We're interacting more on
social media and technology.

00:05:15.683 --> 00:05:17.063
I think there's a lot more pressure

00:05:17.063 --> 00:05:18.803
on students to go to a good school.

00:05:18.803 --> 00:05:20.503
And this kind of meritocracy narrative

00:05:20.503 --> 00:05:22.193
has kind of embedded all of our brains

00:05:22.193 --> 00:05:23.673
in a different way than
when we were in school.

00:05:23.673 --> 00:05:25.273
So, lots of different factors,

00:05:25.273 --> 00:05:27.343
but what we know is like
it is getting worse.

00:05:27.343 --> 00:05:28.993
And so we need some solutions.

00:05:28.993 --> 00:05:29.826
And so that gets to your next question.

00:05:29.826 --> 00:05:30.659
Would you like,

00:05:30.659 --> 00:05:31.492
okay, what are the solutions?

00:05:31.492 --> 00:05:33.333
What are the, you know, what
are the things we can do?

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:36.233
And one of the things the
course teaches is that,

00:05:36.233 --> 00:05:37.743
we were all trying to be happier.

00:05:37.743 --> 00:05:38.883
It's not like people want to feel

00:05:38.883 --> 00:05:40.333
depressed and anxious, right?

00:05:40.333 --> 00:05:42.773
The problem is that we go
about it the wrong way.

00:05:42.773 --> 00:05:44.743
We think we need like the next accolade

00:05:44.743 --> 00:05:46.853
or we need the next, you know paycheck,

00:05:46.853 --> 00:05:48.873
or we need to change our circumstances.

00:05:48.873 --> 00:05:50.563
And the data just suggested that

00:05:50.563 --> 00:05:52.903
that is not the path to higher wellbeing.

00:05:52.903 --> 00:05:55.053
Higher wellbeing comes from our practices.

00:05:55.053 --> 00:05:57.403
A big thing is just social connection.

00:05:57.403 --> 00:06:00.493
You know, carving out time
to spend with family members

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:02.463
and people you care about and love.

00:06:02.463 --> 00:06:04.323
Wellbeing comes from being other oriented

00:06:04.323 --> 00:06:05.383
rather than selfish.

00:06:05.383 --> 00:06:08.153
I think there's this whole
idea for students nowadays,

00:06:08.153 --> 00:06:10.243
that you gotta focus
on you and your grades

00:06:10.243 --> 00:06:12.633
and your accolades and your test scores.

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:14.073
But the data suggests that happiness

00:06:14.073 --> 00:06:15.593
comes from focusing on other people,

00:06:15.593 --> 00:06:17.183
the happiest people out there

00:06:17.183 --> 00:06:18.903
are people who do good for other people.

00:06:18.903 --> 00:06:21.793
And so one of the homework
assignments we give in my classes

00:06:21.793 --> 00:06:24.413
is that students have to do
these random acts of kindness.

00:06:24.413 --> 00:06:25.844
And so, you know, just plug for

00:06:25.844 --> 00:06:28.413
khanacademy.org/donate, right?

00:06:28.413 --> 00:06:30.283
One of the leading predictors of happiness

00:06:30.283 --> 00:06:31.643
is if you donate to good causes.

00:06:31.643 --> 00:06:34.313
So, you know, you can do
something nice for Khan Academy

00:06:34.313 --> 00:06:36.423
and boost your wellbeing.

00:06:36.423 --> 00:06:37.773
But then there's other just simple things

00:06:37.773 --> 00:06:40.293
like healthy habits, just
something as simple as sleep,

00:06:40.293 --> 00:06:41.913
which we know nowadays young people

00:06:41.913 --> 00:06:43.483
are doing less stuff, right?

00:06:43.483 --> 00:06:45.783
The simple act of getting
seven to eight hours of sleep,

00:06:45.783 --> 00:06:47.530
can statistically boost your wellbeing.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.683
You know, people who
are getting five hours

00:06:49.683 --> 00:06:50.613
actually have mood levels.

00:06:50.613 --> 00:06:51.863
They look like they're
clinically depressed

00:06:51.863 --> 00:06:53.453
in public is they're not
getting enough sleep.

00:06:53.453 --> 00:06:57.173
So the answers are really
simple in a funny way.

00:06:57.173 --> 00:06:59.043
They almost seem like kind of trite,

00:06:59.043 --> 00:07:00.743
but actually none of us
are doing them, right?

00:07:00.743 --> 00:07:02.073
And so I think seeing the science,

00:07:02.073 --> 00:07:03.723
when you see a graph that shows,

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:05.093
you know, hey, if you do
random acts of kindness,

00:07:05.093 --> 00:07:05.926
here's your happiness.

00:07:05.926 --> 00:07:07.203
Versus if you don't, it's over here,

00:07:07.203 --> 00:07:09.703
it's like, Oh, it's hard
to argue with those data.

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:13.573
- First of all, our
philanthropy team thanks you.

00:07:13.573 --> 00:07:15.253
Because I think we're gonna take that clip

00:07:15.253 --> 00:07:16.733
of what you just said.

00:07:16.733 --> 00:07:18.643
And actually I would love
to see the graphs too.

00:07:18.643 --> 00:07:22.460
I'm already seeing our next
email ask for donation saying,

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.813
"We're gonna do you a favor by donating.

00:07:24.813 --> 00:07:25.943
We're gonna make you happier.

00:07:25.943 --> 00:07:28.423
And here's the empirical
evidence why that is true."

00:07:28.423 --> 00:07:30.413
'Cause that feels like a win, win.

00:07:30.413 --> 00:07:32.793
'Cause you could imagine,

00:07:32.793 --> 00:07:35.023
you know, sometimes is
hard to ask for money.

00:07:35.023 --> 00:07:37.213
- What's awesome is (inaudible) like tips.

00:07:37.213 --> 00:07:38.046
So many of these tips

00:07:38.046 --> 00:07:39.833
actually have this win-win feature, right?

00:07:39.833 --> 00:07:42.032
Like by the act of doing
something to support yourself

00:07:42.032 --> 00:07:45.483
and your self care, you're
actually helping your community.

00:07:45.483 --> 00:07:47.203
You're helping the people around you.

00:07:47.203 --> 00:07:48.623
And we talk a lot about the evidence

00:07:48.623 --> 00:07:50.313
for emotional contagion, right?

00:07:50.313 --> 00:07:51.943
Like my wellbeing is gonna affect

00:07:51.943 --> 00:07:53.203
the wellbeing of people around me.

00:07:53.203 --> 00:07:56.803
So by like doing the right
sorts of things for yourself,

00:07:56.803 --> 00:07:58.963
that's actually a way that
you can help your friends.

00:07:58.963 --> 00:08:00.883
That's the way you can
help your family members.

00:08:00.883 --> 00:08:03.353
Like it's putting your
oxygen mask on first

00:08:03.353 --> 00:08:04.683
doesn't just give you the oxygen,

00:08:04.683 --> 00:08:07.533
it kind of helps everybody around you too.

00:08:07.533 --> 00:08:08.753
- Laurie, I'm not kidding.

00:08:08.753 --> 00:08:10.303
I think you've just defined our like,

00:08:10.303 --> 00:08:11.823
winter giving campaign.

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:15.683
Like help yourself by helping
the children of the world.

00:08:15.683 --> 00:08:17.323
But going into, I mean,

00:08:17.323 --> 00:08:19.663
there's so much that you
said that I'd love to impact.

00:08:19.663 --> 00:08:21.103
There's already questions coming in.

00:08:21.103 --> 00:08:23.163
And I think this is on
a lot of folks as mine,

00:08:23.163 --> 00:08:27.140
Niche786 on YouTube saying, "How can we,

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.703
oh, this is, oh no, this
is Selena Chang actually.

00:08:29.703 --> 00:08:31.153
I'll get to Niche's question as well.

00:08:31.153 --> 00:08:32.413
You know, what advice do you....

00:08:32.413 --> 00:08:34.113
So you're talking about,
you know, taking your time,

00:08:34.113 --> 00:08:35.143
random acts of kindness,

00:08:35.143 --> 00:08:37.219
getting enough sleep, being present.

00:08:37.219 --> 00:08:39.233
But, you know, the question here is

00:08:39.233 --> 00:08:40.533
how, you know, students need to juggle

00:08:40.533 --> 00:08:42.663
workload and other activities and stress?

00:08:42.663 --> 00:08:44.463
I think there's a lot of
kissing, that sounds nice.

00:08:44.463 --> 00:08:48.183
But I got my, you know, I
got to prepare for the SAT.

00:08:48.183 --> 00:08:50.703
I got this grade, I got this project.

00:08:50.703 --> 00:08:52.153
I got to get my extracurriculars right.

00:08:52.153 --> 00:08:54.663
I mean, right now we're in
college admission season

00:08:54.663 --> 00:08:56.353
for, you know, the early decision.

00:08:56.353 --> 00:08:57.433
And early decision,

00:08:57.433 --> 00:08:59.681
just the words have stress in it.

00:08:59.681 --> 00:09:00.793
- Right, You've just (inaudible).

00:09:00.793 --> 00:09:02.674
- What would you tell this folks.

00:09:02.674 --> 00:09:07.413
- Part of it is honestly
getting your priorities right.

00:09:07.413 --> 00:09:10.103
It's recognizing that you
are not your grades, right?

00:09:10.103 --> 00:09:11.213
You are not your accolades.

00:09:11.213 --> 00:09:13.783
You are not what happens
